var e={level:`B2`,type:`fill-blank`,questions:[{id:`b2-fb-001`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`relative-clauses`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`Use 'who' or 'that' for people in a relative clause.`,wrongReasons:{which:`'which' is used for things, not people.`},text:`The man ___ lives next door is a doctor.`,answers:[[`who`,`that`]]},{id:`b2-fb-002`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`verb-patterns`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`'Look forward to' is followed by the -ing form because 'to' is a preposition.`,wrongReasons:{for:`The correct phrase is 'look forward to'.`},text:`I'm looking forward ___ seeing you.`,answers:[[`to`]]},{id:`b2-fb-003`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`The third conditional uses 'if' + past perfect for an unreal past: If we had booked, we wouldn't have queued.`,wrongReasons:{have:`'If we have booked' is present perfect; an unreal past situation needs 'had booked'.`,would:`Don't put 'would' in the if-clause; use the past perfect 'had booked'.`},text:`If we ___ booked the tickets in advance, we wouldn't have had to queue for two hours.`,answers:[[`had`]]},{id:`b2-fb-004`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`The second conditional uses 'if' + past simple for an imaginary present: If she were more confident, she would apply.`,wrongReasons:{is:`'is' is a real present; with 'would apply' you need the past form 'were/was'.`,"would be":`'would' goes in the main clause, not in the if-clause.`},text:`If she ___ more confident, she would apply for the manager's job.`,answers:[[`were`,`was`]]},{id:`b2-fb-005`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`'Unless' means 'if not': The alarm will go off unless you enter the code (= if you don't enter it).`,wrongReasons:{if:`'if' gives the opposite meaning: the alarm goes off when you do NOT enter the code.`,until:`'until' means 'up to the time when'; here we need a condition meaning 'if not'.`},text:`The alarm will go off ___ you enter the code within thirty seconds.`,answers:[[`unless`]]},{id:`b2-fb-006`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`In the third conditional, 'would have' is followed by the past participle: wouldn't have waited.`,wrongReasons:{wait:`After 'would have' you need the past participle 'waited', not the base form.`,waiting:`'would have waiting' is not a correct verb form; use 'would have waited'.`},text:`If the hospital had had more staff, the patients wouldn't have ___ so long.`,answers:[[`waited`]]},{id:`b2-fb-007`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`'If I were you' is a common second conditional for giving advice: If I were you, I'd complain.`,wrongReasons:{am:`Advice with 'I'd' needs the unreal past: 'If I were you', not 'If I am you'.`,"had been":`'had been' refers to the past; advice about now uses 'If I were you'.`},text:`If I ___ you, I'd speak to the landlord about the broken heating.`,answers:[[`were`,`was`]]},{id:`b2-fb-008`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`The zero conditional (general truths) uses the present simple in both clauses: If you heat ice, it melts.`,wrongReasons:{heated:`General scientific facts use the present simple, not the past: 'if you heat it'.`,"will heat":`Don't use 'will' in the if-clause, especially for general truths.`},text:`Water boils if you ___ it to 100 degrees Celsius.`,answers:[[`heat`,`bring`]]},{id:`b2-fb-009`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`A mixed conditional links a past condition to a present result: If he had taken the job, he'd be living there now.`,wrongReasons:{been:`'he'd been living' is past perfect; for a present result use 'would be living'.`,have:`'would have living' is not grammatical; use 'would be living' for a result now.`},text:`If he had taken the job in Tokyo, he'd ___ living in Japan now.`,answers:[[`be`]]},{id:`b2-fb-010`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`In formal English you can drop 'if' and invert: Had I known = If I had known.`,wrongReasons:{if:`'If I known' is incomplete; you need 'If I had known' or the inversion 'Had I known'.`,have:`Inversion in the third conditional uses 'had', not 'have'.`},text:`___ I known the museum was closed on Mondays, I wouldn't have gone there.`,answers:[[`had`]]},{id:`b2-fb-011`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`'Provided (that)' means 'only if': Provided that you return it on time, it's free.`,wrongReasons:{if:`'provided if' is wrong; the expression is 'provided (that)'.`,than:`'than' is used in comparisons, not in the expression 'provided that'.`},text:`Provided ___ you return the bike by six, you won't have to pay extra.`,answers:[[`that`]]},{id:`b2-fb-012`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`'As long as' means 'only if': You can go out as long as you're home by ten.`,wrongReasons:{that:`The expression is 'as long as', not 'as long that'.`,so:`'so' doesn't complete this expression; say 'as long as'.`},text:`I'll lend you my car as long ___ you drive carefully.`,answers:[[`as`]]},{id:`b2-fb-013`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`The first conditional uses 'if' + present tense and 'will' in the main clause: If it stops raining, we'll go out.`,wrongReasons:{"will stop":`Don't use 'will' in the if-clause; use the present: 'if it stops'.`,stopped:`The past 'stopped' belongs to the second conditional and doesn't match 'we'll go'.`},text:`If it ___ raining by the afternoon, we'll go for a walk along the river.`,answers:[[`stops`,`has stopped`]]},{id:`b2-fb-014`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`This mixed conditional has a past condition (had invested) and a present result (wouldn't be struggling).`,wrongReasons:{has:`'years ago' shows an unreal past, so you need the past perfect 'had invested'.`,"would have":`'would' doesn't go in the if-clause; use 'had invested'.`},text:`If the company ___ invested in new technology years ago, it wouldn't be struggling now.`,answers:[[`had`]]},{id:`b2-fb-015`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`The second conditional describes unlikely future situations with the past simple: If you won, what would you do?`,wrongReasons:{win:`With 'would you do', the if-clause needs the past simple 'won'.`,"will win":`'will' is not used in the if-clause; say 'if you won'.`},text:`What would you do if you ___ the lottery tomorrow?`,answers:[[`won`]]},{id:`b2-fb-016`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`'In case' means 'as a precaution because something might happen': Take a map in case you get lost.`,wrongReasons:{if:`'in if' is not English; the expression is 'in case'.`,order:`'in order' is used for purpose with 'to' or 'that', not here.`},text:`Pack a warm jumper in ___ the evenings get cold in the mountains.`,answers:[[`case`]]},{id:`b2-fb-017`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`Third conditional: 'if' + past perfect, here negative: if she hadn't overslept.`,wrongReasons:{"didn't":`An unreal past condition needs the past perfect 'hadn't overslept', not the past simple.`,"wouldn't":`'would' is used in the main clause, not after 'if'.`},text:`She would have caught the train if she ___ overslept.`,answers:[[`hadn't`,`had not`]]},{id:`b2-fb-018`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`Third conditional: 'would (not) have' + past participle in the result: If I had left earlier, I wouldn't have been late.`,wrongReasons:{"won't":`'won't have been' is a future form; an unreal past result needs 'wouldn't have'.`,"didn't":`'didn't have been' is not grammatical; use 'wouldn't have been'.`},text:`If I hadn't missed the bus this morning, I ___ have been late for the interview.`,answers:[[`wouldn't`,`would not`,`might not`]]},{id:`b2-fb-019`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`'Supposing' works like 'if' and takes the past for imaginary situations: Supposing you were offered it, would you accept?`,wrongReasons:{are:`With 'would you accept', the condition needs the past form 'were offered'.`,"will be":`Don't use 'will' after 'supposing'; it works like 'if'.`},text:`Supposing you ___ offered the job, would you accept it?`,answers:[[`were`,`got`]]},{id:`b2-fb-020`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`'Even if' means 'whether or not this happens': Even if it's cold, we'll swim.`,wrongReasons:{though:`'even though' is for real facts; for a possible future condition use 'even if'.`,when:`'even when' describes repeated situations, not one possible future event.`},text:`Even ___ it rains tomorrow, the match will go ahead.`,answers:[[`if`]]},{id:`b2-fb-021`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`reported-speech`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`In reported speech the present often moves back one tense: 'I am tired' becomes 'she said she was tired'.`,wrongReasons:{is:`The situation was yesterday evening, so 'am' moves back to 'was' in reported speech.`,were:`With 'she', the past of 'be' is 'was', not 'were'.`},text:`Yesterday evening Anna said that she ___ too tired to go out.`,answers:[[`was`]]},{id:`b2-fb-022`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`reported-speech`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`Reported questions use statement word order and move tenses back: 'Where did you live?' becomes 'where I had lived'.`,wrongReasons:{did:`Reported questions don't use 'did'; use statement order and the past perfect.`,have:`After 'asked' the tense moves back, so 'have lived' becomes 'had lived'.`},text:`He asked me where I ___ lived before moving to Rome.`,answers:[[`had`]]},{id:`b2-fb-023`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`reported-speech`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`Negative reported orders use 'tell somebody not to do': She told me not to shout.`,wrongReasons:{"don't":`In reported orders use 'not to' + verb, not 'don't'.`,no:`'no' is not used before an infinitive; say 'not to talk'.`},text:`The teacher told us ___ to talk during the exam.`,answers:[[`not`]]},{id:`b2-fb-024`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`reported-speech`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`Reported yes/no questions are introduced by 'if' or 'whether': He asked if I was ready.`,wrongReasons:{that:`'that' introduces reported statements, not yes/no questions.`,what:`'what' asks for information; this is a yes/no question, so use 'if' or 'whether'.`},text:`She asked me ___ I had ever been to Scotland.`,answers:[[`if`,`whether`]]},{id:`b2-fb-025`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`reported-speech`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`In reported speech 'will' changes to 'would': 'I'll call you' became 'he said he would call me'.`,wrongReasons:{will:`After a past reporting verb, 'will' usually becomes 'would'.`,can:`'can' changes the meaning to ability; the promise 'I'll call' becomes 'would call'.`},text:`My brother told me he ___ call me the next day, but he forgot.`,answers:[[`would`]]},{id:`b2-fb-026`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`reported-speech`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`'Must' for obligation often becomes 'had to' in reported speech: He said he had to go.`,wrongReasons:{must:`'must to' is never correct; in reported speech use 'had to'.`,have:`The reporting verb is in the past, so 'have to' moves back to 'had to'.`},text:`'We must leave now,' said Tom. Tom said that they ___ to leave immediately.`,answers:[[`had`,`needed`]]},{id:`b2-fb-027`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`reported-speech`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`'Explain' needs 'to' before the person: She explained to me that... (not 'explained me').`,wrongReasons:{for:`We explain something 'to' a person, not 'for' a person.`,at:`'at' is not used with 'explain'; the correct preposition is 'to'.`},text:`She explained ___ us that the flight had been delayed.`,answers:[[`to`]]},{id:`b2-fb-028`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`reported-speech`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`Reported questions move the tense back and use statement order: 'Why did you resign?' becomes 'why he had resigned'.`,wrongReasons:{did:`Reported questions use statement order, so 'did' is not used.`,has:`After 'asked' the present perfect moves back to the past perfect 'had resigned'.`},text:`The journalist asked the minister why he ___ resigned.`,answers:[[`had`]]},{id:`b2-fb-029`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`reported-speech`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`'Warn somebody not to do something' reports a negative warning: She warned me not to be late.`,wrongReasons:{"don't":`Reported warnings use 'not to' + verb, not 'don't'.`,no:`'no to touch' is incorrect; the negative infinitive is 'not to touch'.`},text:`'Don't touch the paintings,' the guide said. The guide warned us ___ to touch the paintings.`,answers:[[`not`]]},{id:`b2-fb-030`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`reported-speech`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`Indirect questions use statement word order: Where is it? becomes Could you tell me where it is?`,wrongReasons:{"is it":`In indirect questions the subject comes before the verb: 'where the station is'.`,does:`'does' is not needed in an indirect question; use the verb 'is' after the subject.`},text:`Could you tell me where the nearest station ___?`,answers:[[`is`]]},{id:`b2-fb-031`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`reported-speech`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`'Admit' is followed by '-ing' (or a that-clause): He admitted having lied.`,wrongReasons:{to:`'admitted to taken' is not grammatical; you need 'admitted (to) having taken'.`,had:`After 'admit' use '-ing' ('having taken') or 'that he had taken'.`},text:`He admitted ___ taken the money from the drawer.`,answers:[[`having`]]},{id:`b2-fb-032`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`reported-speech`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`'Advise somebody to do something' reports advice: She advised me to rest.`,wrongReasons:{that:`'advise me that' would need a full clause with a subject; here we need 'advise me to'.`,for:`'advise' is not followed by 'for' before a verb; use 'to eat'.`},text:`My doctor advised me ___ eat less sugar.`,answers:[[`to`]]},{id:`b2-fb-033`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`reported-speech`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`Reported choice questions use 'whether' or 'if': She asked whether I preferred tea or coffee.`,wrongReasons:{what:`This is a choice between two options, so report it with 'whether' or 'if'.`,that:`'that' introduces statements, not questions.`},text:`They asked us ___ we wanted tea or coffee.`,answers:[[`whether`,`if`]]},{id:`b2-fb-034`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`reported-speech`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`In reported speech 'ago' usually becomes 'before' or 'earlier': 'two days ago' becomes 'two days before'.`,wrongReasons:{ago:`'ago' refers to now; when the time is shifted to the past, use 'before' or 'earlier'.`,later:`'later' changes the meaning; the meeting happened before she spoke.`},text:`She told me she had met him two days ___.`,answers:[[`before`,`earlier`]]},{id:`b2-fb-035`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`reported-speech`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`In reported speech 'tomorrow' becomes 'the next day' or 'the following day'.`,wrongReasons:{tomorrow:`'the tomorrow day' is not English; 'tomorrow' becomes 'the next day'.`,after:`'the after day' is incorrect; say 'the next day' or 'the following day'.`},text:`He said he would see me the ___ day at the conference.`,answers:[[`next`,`following`]]},{id:`b2-fb-036`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`reported-speech`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`The present perfect moves back to the past perfect in reported speech: 'I've lost' becomes 'he had lost'.`,wrongReasons:{has:`After 'said' the present perfect 'has lost' usually becomes 'had lost'.`,have:`'have' doesn't agree with 'he', and the tense should move back to 'had'.`},text:`'I've lost my keys,' said Ben. Ben said that he ___ lost his keys.`,answers:[[`had`]]},{id:`b2-fb-037`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`reported-speech`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`'Order somebody to do something' reports a command: The coach ordered us to run.`,wrongReasons:{that:`'ordered the driver that get out' is wrong; use 'order somebody to do'.`,for:`'order' is not followed by 'for' before a verb; use 'to'.`},text:`The police officer ordered the driver ___ get out of the car.`,answers:[[`to`]]},{id:`b2-fb-038`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`reported-speech`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`'Suggest' is followed by '-ing' or a that-clause, never by the infinitive: He suggested taking a taxi.`,wrongReasons:{"to go":`'suggest' is not followed by 'to' + verb; use 'suggested going'.`,go:`After 'suggested' without a subject, use the -ing form 'going'.`},text:`She suggested ___ to the beach for the weekend.`,answers:[[`going`]]},{id:`b2-fb-039`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`The past simple passive is 'was/were' + past participle: The school was built in 1990.`,wrongReasons:{is:`'last spring' is finished past time, so use 'was opened'.`,has:`'has opened' is active and doesn't go with 'last spring'; use 'was opened'.`},text:`The new bridge ___ opened by the mayor last spring.`,answers:[[`was`]]},{id:`b2-fb-040`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`The future passive is 'will be' + past participle: The winners will be chosen tomorrow.`,wrongReasons:{been:`After 'will' use the base form 'be', not 'been'.`,being:`'will being' is not correct; the future passive is 'will be announced'.`},text:`The results will ___ announced on Friday.`,answers:[[`be`]]},{id:`b2-fb-041`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`The present continuous passive is 'am/is/are being' + past participle: The car is being washed.`,wrongReasons:{has:`'has being' is not a verb form; the present continuous passive is 'is being'.`,be:`'The road be being repaired' is not correct; the verb must agree with 'road': 'is being'.`},text:`The road ___ being repaired, so expect delays this week.`,answers:[[`is`]]},{id:`b2-fb-042`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`The present perfect passive is 'have/has been' + past participle: The email hasn't been sent yet.`,wrongReasons:{be:`After 'hasn't' you need 'been', not 'be'.`,being:`'hasn't being' is not correct; use 'hasn't been caught'.`},text:`The thief hasn't ___ caught yet.`,answers:[[`been`]]},{id:`b2-fb-043`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`Modal passive: modal + 'be' + past participle: Bags must be checked.`,wrongReasons:{been:`After 'must' use 'be', not 'been' (unless it is 'must have been').`,being:`'must being' is incorrect; the modal passive is 'must be switched'.`},text:`Mobile phones must ___ switched off during the flight.`,answers:[[`be`]]},{id:`b2-fb-044`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`'Be said / believed / thought to + infinitive' reports general opinion: He is said to be very rich.`,wrongReasons:{that:`'is said that be' is wrong; use 'is said to be' or 'It is said that it is...'.`,it:`'is said it be' is not grammatical; the structure is 'is said to be'.`},text:`The castle is said ___ be haunted, but nobody has ever seen a ghost there.`,answers:[[`to`]]},{id:`b2-fb-045`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`For past events use 'to have been' + past participle: He is thought to have been injured.`,wrongReasons:{be:`After 'to have' use 'been', not 'be'.`,being:`'to have being' is not a correct form; use 'to have been'.`},text:`The painting is believed to have ___ stolen in the 1990s.`,answers:[[`been`]]},{id:`b2-fb-046`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`The causative 'have/get something done' means someone else does it for you: I get my hair cut every month.`,wrongReasons:{makes:`'make' doesn't form the causative with a past participle; use 'have' or 'get'.`,does:`'does her car serviced' is wrong; the causative uses 'has' or 'gets'.`},text:`She ___ her car serviced every winter.`,answers:[[`gets`,`has`]]},{id:`b2-fb-047`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`The present simple passive is 'am/is/are' + past participle: Coffee is grown in Brazil.`,wrongReasons:{are:`'English' is singular, so use 'is spoken'.`,speaks:`English doesn't speak; people speak it, so the passive 'is spoken' is needed.`},text:`Today English ___ spoken in many countries around the world.`,answers:[[`is`]]},{id:`b2-fb-048`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`Use 'was/were' + past participle for finished past actions: The tower was built in 1889.`,wrongReasons:{is:`'in 1885' is finished past time, so use 'was built'.`,"has been":`The present perfect doesn't go with a past date like 'in 1885'.`},text:`The museum ___ built in 1885, but it was badly damaged during the war.`,answers:[[`was`]]},{id:`b2-fb-049`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`The past perfect passive is 'had been' + past participle: The food had been eaten.`,wrongReasons:{be:`After 'had' use 'been', not 'be'.`,being:`'had being' is not a verb form; use 'had been sold'.`},text:`By the time we arrived, all the tickets had ___ sold.`,answers:[[`been`]]},{id:`b2-fb-050`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`With verbs like give, send and offer, the passive keeps 'to' before the person: The letter was sent to me.`,wrongReasons:{for:`We give something 'to' someone, so the passive is 'was given to'.`,at:`'at' is not used with 'give'; the correct preposition is 'to'.`},text:`The prize was given ___ the youngest student in the class.`,answers:[[`to`]]},{id:`b2-fb-051`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`The passive -ing form is 'being' + past participle: I don't like being interrupted.`,wrongReasons:{be:`After 'hates' use the -ing form: 'being told'.`,been:`'hates been' is not grammatical; use 'being told'.`},text:`He hates ___ told what to do.`,answers:[[`being`]]},{id:`b2-fb-052`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`Passive infinitive: 'to be' + past participle: The road is expected to be closed.`,wrongReasons:{been:`After 'to' use the base form 'be' ('to be cancelled').`,being:`'to being cancelled' is wrong here; 'expect' takes 'to be cancelled'.`},text:`The concert is expected to ___ cancelled because of the storm.`,answers:[[`be`]]},{id:`b2-fb-053`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`Use the past simple passive for a finished past event: Our train was cancelled.`,wrongReasons:{is:`The story is in the past (we missed), so use 'was delayed'.`,has:`'has delayed' is active; the flight didn't delay something, it 'was delayed'.`},text:`Our flight ___ delayed for three hours, so we missed our connection.`,answers:[[`was`,`got`]]},{id:`b2-fb-054`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`'Need + -ing' has a passive meaning: The car needs washing (= needs to be washed).`,wrongReasons:{clean:`After 'need' use '-ing' ('need cleaning') or the passive infinitive 'to be cleaned'.`,cleaned:`'need cleaned' is not standard English; say 'need cleaning' or 'need to be cleaned'.`},text:`The windows are so dirty; they really need ___.`,answers:[[`cleaning`,`to be cleaned`,`washing`,`to be washed`,`a clean`,`a wash`]]},{id:`b2-fb-055`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`'Money' is uncountable and takes a singular verb: A lot of money has been wasted.`,wrongReasons:{have:`'money' is uncountable, so it takes 'has', not 'have'.`,is:`'is been' is not a verb form; the present perfect passive is 'has been spent'.`},text:`A lot of money ___ been spent on the new stadium already.`,answers:[[`has`]]},{id:`b2-fb-056`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`Active 'make somebody do' becomes passive 'be made to do': They were made to wait.`,wrongReasons:{for:`'were made for apologise' is wrong; the passive of 'make' takes 'to' + verb.`,that:`'were made that apologise' is not grammatical; use 'were made to apologise'.`},text:`The children were made ___ apologise to their teacher.`,answers:[[`to`]]},{id:`b2-fb-057`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`relative-clauses`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`'Whose' shows possession in relative clauses: The man whose dog barks lives here.`,wrongReasons:{"who's":`'who's' means 'who is'; for possession use 'whose'.`,who:`'who car' is wrong; you need the possessive 'whose car'.`},text:`The woman ___ car was stolen called the police.`,answers:[[`whose`]]},{id:`b2-fb-058`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`relative-clauses`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`Use 'where' (= in which) for places in relative clauses: Rome, where I studied, is beautiful.`,wrongReasons:{which:`'which I lived' would need 'in' ('which I lived in'); use 'where'.`,that:`'that' can't introduce an extra-information clause after a comma.`},text:`Paris, ___ I lived for three years, is still my favourite city.`,answers:[[`where`,`in which`]]},{id:`b2-fb-059`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`relative-clauses`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`Clauses with extra information (with commas) use 'who' for people, never 'that': My aunt, who lives in Leeds, is a nurse.`,wrongReasons:{that:`'that' can't be used in extra-information clauses (between commas) between commas.`,which:`'which' is for things, not people.`},text:`My grandfather, ___ is 90, still goes swimming every day.`,answers:[[`who`]]},{id:`b2-fb-060`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`relative-clauses`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`Use 'which' or 'that' for things in defining relative clauses: This is the film that I liked.`,wrongReasons:{who:`'who' is for people, not things.`,what:`'what' means 'the thing that' and can't follow a noun like 'the book'.`},text:`This is the book ___ I told you about.`,answers:[[`which`,`that`]]},{id:`b2-fb-061`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`relative-clauses`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`'Which' after a comma can refer to the whole previous clause: She was late, which annoyed me.`,wrongReasons:{what:`'what' can't refer back to a whole clause; use ', which'.`,that:`'that' can't be used after a comma to refer to a whole clause.`},text:`He failed the exam, ___ surprised everyone.`,answers:[[`which`]]},{id:`b2-fb-062`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`relative-clauses`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`After 'the reason' use 'why' or 'that': The reason why I called is simple.`,wrongReasons:{because:`'the reason because' is incorrect; say 'the reason why'.`,which:`'the reason which she left' is wrong; use 'why', 'that' or 'for which'.`},text:`The reason ___ she left is still a mystery.`,answers:[[`why`,`that`,`for which`]]},{id:`b2-fb-063`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`relative-clauses`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`Use 'when' (or 'that') after time nouns: I remember the year when we moved.`,wrongReasons:{where:`'where' is for places, not times like 'the day'.`,which:`'which we first met' needs a preposition ('on which'); use 'when'.`},text:`I'll never forget the day ___ we first met.`,answers:[[`when`,`that`,`on which`]]},{id:`b2-fb-064`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`relative-clauses`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`After a preposition, use 'whom' for people: the person with whom I work.`,wrongReasons:{who:`After a preposition like 'to', formal English uses 'whom', not 'who'.`,that:`'that' can never come directly after a preposition.`},text:`The people to ___ I spoke were very helpful.`,answers:[[`whom`]]},{id:`b2-fb-065`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`relative-clauses`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`'Most of whose' shows possession in an extra-information clause: the club, many of whose members are old.`,wrongReasons:{who:`'most of who employees' is wrong; the employees belong to the company, so use 'whose'.`,which:`'most of which employees' is incorrect; for possession use 'whose'.`},text:`The company, most of ___ employees work from home, has closed its main office.`,answers:[[`whose`]]},{id:`b2-fb-066`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`relative-clauses`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`Use 'all of / some of / both of whom' for people in extra-information clauses (between commas): two sons, both of whom live abroad.`,wrongReasons:{them:`'all of them' would need a new sentence or 'and'; in a relative clause use 'all of whom'.`,who:`After 'of' you need the object form 'whom'.`},text:`She has three brothers, all of ___ are doctors.`,answers:[[`whom`]]},{id:`b2-fb-067`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`relative-clauses`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`When a preposition stays at the end of the clause, use 'which' or 'that': the house which we lived in.`,wrongReasons:{where:`With 'in' at the end, use 'which' or 'that'; 'where' already means 'in which'.`,who:`'who' is for people, not places like a hotel.`},text:`That's the hotel ___ we stayed in last summer.`,answers:[[`that`,`which`]]},{id:`b2-fb-068`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`relative-clauses`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`'What' means 'the thing that' and starts a clause on its own: What I need is a holiday.`,wrongReasons:{which:`'Which' needs a noun before it to refer to; use 'What' (= the thing that).`,that:`'That surprised me most was...' is not grammatical; use 'What'.`},text:`___ surprised me most was how calm she stayed.`,answers:[[`what`]]},{id:`b2-fb-069`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`relative-clauses`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`Use 'who' or 'that' for people in defining relative clauses: the nurse who helped me.`,wrongReasons:{which:`'which' is for things, not people.`,whose:`'whose' shows possession and needs a noun after it.`},text:`The scientist ___ discovered the vaccine received a major award.`,answers:[[`who`,`that`]]},{id:`b2-fb-070`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`relative-clauses`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`After a preposition use 'which' for things and places: the box in which I keep my letters.`,wrongReasons:{where:`'in where' is incorrect; say 'in which' or just 'where'.`,that:`'that' can't follow a preposition such as 'in'.`},text:`We visited a village in ___ nobody had a car.`,answers:[[`which`]]},{id:`b2-fb-071`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`relative-clauses`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`Use 'who' or 'that' after 'anyone', 'someone', 'people': Anyone who is late will miss the bus.`,wrongReasons:{which:`'which' is for things; 'anyone' refers to people.`,whom:`'whom' is an object form; here the pronoun is the subject of 'wants'.`},text:`Anyone ___ wants to join the trip should sign up by Friday.`,answers:[[`who`,`that`]]},{id:`b2-fb-072`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`relative-clauses`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`Use 'where' for places when the clause is complete: the café where we met.`,wrongReasons:{which:`'which I can buy stamps' would need a preposition ('at which'); use 'where'.`,that:`'that I can buy stamps' is incomplete; for a place use 'where'.`},text:`Is there a shop near here ___ I can buy stamps?`,answers:[[`where`,`at which`]]},{id:`b2-fb-073`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`relative-clauses`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`Clauses with extra information about things use 'which', never 'that': The laptop, which is new, is broken.`,wrongReasons:{that:`'that' can't be used in an extra-information clause between commas.`,who:`'who' is for people, not things like a car.`},text:`The car, ___ was parked outside the bank, had been stolen.`,answers:[[`which`]]},{id:`b2-fb-074`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`relative-clauses`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`'Whose' + noun shows possession: the girl whose bike was stolen.`,wrongReasons:{who:`'who homework' is wrong; the homework belongs to the students, so use 'whose'.`,"who's":`'who's' means 'who is', which makes 'who is homework is' meaningless.`},text:`Students ___ homework is late will lose marks.`,answers:[[`whose`]]},{id:`b2-fb-075`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`wish`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`'Wish + could' expresses a wish about an ability you don't have: I wish I could drive.`,wrongReasons:{can:`After 'wish' move back to the past: 'could', not 'can'.`,would:`We don't use 'would' after 'I wish I'; for ability use 'could'.`},text:`I wish I ___ speak Japanese.`,answers:[[`could`]]},{id:`b2-fb-076`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`wish`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`'Wish + past perfect' expresses regret about the past: I wish I hadn't said that.`,wrongReasons:{"didn't":`For regrets about the past, use the past perfect 'hadn't eaten'.`,"haven't":`'wish' about the past needs 'hadn't', not the present perfect.`},text:`I wish I ___ eaten so much at dinner. I feel sick now.`,answers:[[`hadn't`,`had not`]]},{id:`b2-fb-077`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`wish`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`'If only / wish + would' shows annoyance or a wish for change: I wish the bus would come.`,wrongReasons:{will:`After 'if only' 'will' moves back to 'would'.`,can:`'can' expresses ability and doesn't follow 'if only' in this way; use 'would'.`},text:`If only it ___ stop raining!`,answers:[[`would`]]},{id:`b2-fb-078`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`wish`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`'Wish + past simple' expresses a wish about a present situation: I wish I were richer.`,wrongReasons:{is:`After 'wish' use the past ('were/was') for an unreal present.`,"would be":`We don't use 'would' for a wish about our own present state; use 'were'.`},text:`She wishes she ___ taller.`,answers:[[`were`,`was`]]},{id:`b2-fb-079`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`wish`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`'Wish + somebody + would' is used to complain about other people's behaviour: I wish he would listen.`,wrongReasons:{will:`After 'wish' use 'would', not 'will'.`,to:`'wish you to stop' is a very formal request; to complain about someone's behaviour, say 'I wish you would stop'.`},text:`I wish you ___ stop interrupting me when I'm talking.`,answers:[[`would`]]},{id:`b2-fb-080`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`wish`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`'If only + past perfect' expresses strong regret about the past: If only I had studied.`,wrongReasons:{have:`Regrets about the past need the past perfect 'had listened'.`,did:`'did listened' is not grammatical; use 'had listened'.`},text:`If only I ___ listened to my parents' advice.`,answers:[[`had`]]},{id:`b2-fb-081`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`wish`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`'It's time + subject + past simple' says something should happen now: It's time you went to bed.`,wrongReasons:{go:`After 'It's time we...' use the past simple: 'It's time we went'.`,"will go":`'will' is not used after 'It's time we'; use the past simple.`},text:`It's time we ___ home; it's getting late.`,answers:[[`went`,`got`,`headed`]]},{id:`b2-fb-082`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`wish`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`'Would rather + somebody + past simple' expresses a preference about someone else: I'd rather you stayed.`,wrongReasons:{"don't":`After 'I'd rather you' use the past simple 'didn't', not the present.`,not:`'I'd rather you not smoke' is not standard British English; use 'didn't smoke'.`},text:`I'd rather you ___ smoke in the house.`,answers:[[`didn't`,`did not`]]},{id:`b2-fb-083`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`wish`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`After 'wish + had(n't)' use the past participle: I wish I hadn't bought it.`,wrongReasons:{spend:`After 'hadn't' you need the past participle 'spent'.`,spended:`'spend' is irregular: the past participle is 'spent'.`},text:`I wish I hadn't ___ so much money on clothes last month.`,answers:[[`spent`]]},{id:`b2-fb-084`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`wish`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`'Wish + past simple' is used for present situations we would like to be different: I wish I lived nearer.`,wrongReasons:{live:`After 'wish' use the past simple 'lived' for an unreal present.`,"would live":`We don't use 'would' after 'wish' for our own situation; use 'lived'.`},text:`Do you ever wish you ___ by the sea instead of in the city centre?`,answers:[[`lived`,`were living`]]},{id:`b2-fb-085`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`wish`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`'Wish + past simple' expresses a wish about the present or near future: I wish I didn't have an exam.`,wrongReasons:{"don't":`After 'wish' move the present back: 'didn't have to'.`,"won't":`'will' is not used after 'I wish I'; use 'didn't'.`},text:`I wish I ___ have to work this weekend.`,answers:[[`didn't`,`did not`]]},{id:`b2-fb-086`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`wish`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`Regret about the past: wish + past perfect. She wishes she had gone to the party.`,wrongReasons:{has:`After 'wish' a past regret needs 'had accepted', not 'has accepted'.`,would:`'would accepted' is not grammatical; use 'had accepted'.`},text:`He wishes he ___ accepted the job offer in London.`,answers:[[`had`]]},{id:`b2-fb-087`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`wish`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`'If only + could' expresses a wish about ability right now: If only I could find my keys!`,wrongReasons:{can:`After 'if only' use the past form 'could'.`,will:`'will' doesn't follow 'if only' here; for ability use 'could'.`},text:`If only I ___ remember where I put my passport!`,answers:[[`could`]]},{id:`b2-fb-088`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`wish`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`Use 'wish + wouldn't' to complain about other people's annoying habits: I wish he wouldn't shout.`,wrongReasons:{"won't":`After 'wish', 'won't' becomes 'wouldn't'.`,"don't":`'don't be so noisy' is an order; after 'wish' use 'wouldn't be'.`},text:`I wish my neighbours ___ be so noisy at night.`,answers:[[`wouldn't`,`would not`]]},{id:`b2-fb-089`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`wish`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`'If only + past simple' expresses a wish about the present: If only I had a car.`,wrongReasons:{have:`After 'if only' use the past simple 'had' for an unreal present.`,"would have":`'would have' doesn't follow 'if only' for a present wish; use 'had'.`},text:`If only we ___ more time, we could visit the castle too.`,answers:[[`had`]]},{id:`b2-fb-090`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`wish`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`'Would rather + base form' expresses your own preference: I'd rather walk than drive.`,wrongReasons:{staying:`After 'would rather' use the base form 'stay', not the -ing form.`,"to stay":`'would rather' is followed by the base form without 'to'.`},text:`I'd rather ___ at home tonight than go to the party.`,answers:[[`stay`,`be`,`remain`]]},{id:`b2-fb-091`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`wish`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`'Wish + past perfect' for regret about the past also works in questions: Do you wish you had left earlier?`,wrongReasons:{have:`'earlier' shows a past regret, so use 'had known'.`,would:`'would known' is not a correct verb form; use 'had known'.`},text:`Don't you wish you ___ known about this restaurant earlier?`,answers:[[`had`]]},{id:`b2-fb-092`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`wish`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`'Wish + past simple' expresses dissatisfaction with a present situation: I wish it wasn't so cold.`,wrongReasons:{"isn't":`After 'wish' move the present back: 'wasn't' or 'weren't'.`,"won't be":`'will' is not used after 'wish' for a present situation; use 'wasn't'.`},text:`I wish it ___ so expensive to rent a flat in this city.`,answers:[[`weren't`,`wasn't`,`were not`,`was not`]]},{id:`b2-fb-093`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`Use 'can't' for a negative deduction when you are sure something is impossible: He can't be hungry; he just ate.`,wrongReasons:{"mustn't":`'mustn't' means something is forbidden; for a negative deduction use 'can't'.`,must:`The evidence shows she is not at home, so 'must' gives the wrong meaning.`},text:`She ___ be at home. I've just seen her at the gym.`,answers:[[`can't`,`cannot`,`couldn't`,`could not`]]},{id:`b2-fb-094`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`'Must have + past participle' is a confident deduction about the past: She must have left early.`,wrongReasons:{can:`'can have' isn't used for past deductions; when you're sure, use 'must have'.`,should:`'should have' talks about what was right to do, not a deduction.`},text:`The lights are off and nobody's answering the phone. They ___ have gone out, I'm sure of it.`,answers:[[`must`]]},{id:`b2-fb-095`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`'Should / could have + past participle' criticises a past action that didn't happen: You should have called.`,wrongReasons:{must:`'must have' expresses deduction, not criticism.`,would:`'would have told' describes a hypothetical result, not a complaint about what you didn't do.`},text:`You ___ have told me you were coming! I would have cooked something.`,answers:[[`should`,`could`,`might`]]},{id:`b2-fb-096`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`'Needn't / don't have to' mean it is not necessary: You needn't come early.`,wrongReasons:{"mustn't":`'mustn't' means it is forbidden; here it is simply not necessary.`,"can't":`'can't' means you are not allowed or not able, which is not the idea here.`},text:`You ___ take an umbrella; it's not going to rain.`,answers:[[`needn't`,`need not`,`don't have to`,`do not have to`,`don't need to`,`do not need to`]]},{id:`b2-fb-097`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`'Needn't have + past participle' means you did something that wasn't necessary: I needn't have cooked so much.`,wrongReasons:{"mustn't":`'mustn't have' is not used for unnecessary past actions; use 'needn't have'.`,"didn't":`'didn't have hurried' is not grammatical; use 'needn't have hurried'.`},text:`We ___ have hurried to the station; the train was delayed anyway, so all that rushing was unnecessary.`,answers:[[`needn't`,`need not`]]},{id:`b2-fb-098`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`'Could' expresses general ability in the past: My grandmother could speak four languages.`,wrongReasons:{can:`The time is in the past, so use 'could', not 'can'.`,might:`'might' expresses possibility, not ability.`},text:`When I was six, I ___ already swim fifty metres.`,answers:[[`could`]]},{id:`b2-fb-099`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`Use 'was/were able to' for a specific success in the past: I was able to fix it.`,wrongReasons:{could:`'could able' is not English; use 'were able to'.`,was:`The subject is 'we', so the verb is 'were'.`},text:`After hours of searching, we finally ___ able to find the missing cat.`,answers:[[`were`]]},{id:`b2-fb-100`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`'Mustn't / can't' express prohibition: You mustn't park here.`,wrongReasons:{"don't have to":`'don't have to' means it isn't necessary, but here smoking is forbidden.`,"needn't":`'needn't' means it isn't necessary; we need a word that means 'not allowed'.`},text:`You ___ smoke in here. It's a hospital!`,answers:[[`mustn't`,`must not`,`can't`,`cannot`]]},{id:`b2-fb-101`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`'Can't / couldn't have + past participle' is a negative deduction about the past: She can't have heard me.`,wrongReasons:{"mustn't":`'mustn't have' is not used for negative deductions; use 'can't have'.`,must:`'must have left' contradicts the evidence that he is still here.`},text:`I'm sure he's still here: he ___ have left yet, because his coat and bag are on his chair.`,answers:[[`can't`,`cannot`,`couldn't`,`could not`]]},{id:`b2-fb-102`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`'Might / may / could' express possibility when you are not sure: He might be ill.`,wrongReasons:{must:`'must' shows certainty, but the speaker is not sure.`,can:`'can' is not used for possibility in a specific present situation.`},text:`I'm not sure where Lisa is. She ___ be in the library, or maybe in the canteen.`,answers:[[`might`,`may`,`could`]]},{id:`b2-fb-103`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`'Had better + base form' gives strong advice: You had better hurry.`,wrongReasons:{would:`The expression is 'had better', not 'would better'.`,should:`'should better' is not English; say 'had better' or just 'should'.`},text:`You ___ better see a doctor about that cough.`,answers:[[`had`]]},{id:`b2-fb-104`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`'Had to' expresses past obligation ('must' has no past form): I had to get up early.`,wrongReasons:{must:`'must' has no past form and is never followed by 'to'; use 'had to'.`,have:`The situation is in the past, so use 'had to', not 'have to'.`},text:`I ___ to wear a uniform at my old school, but now I can wear what I like.`,answers:[[`had`,`used`]]},{id:`b2-fb-105`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`'May / might / could have + past participle' express a possibility about the past: He may have forgotten.`,wrongReasons:{must:`'must have' shows certainty, but the speaker says 'I'm not sure'.`,can:`'can have' is not used for past possibility; use 'may/might/could have'.`},text:`I'm not sure, but she ___ have missed the bus.`,answers:[[`may`,`might`,`could`]]},{id:`b2-fb-106`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`'Ought to have + past participle' means it would have been good to do something: You ought to have come.`,wrongReasons:{should:`'should' is not followed by 'to'; say 'should have seen' or 'ought to have seen'.`,must:`'must' is never followed by 'to'.`},text:`You ___ to have seen the view from the top. It was amazing!`,answers:[[`ought`]]},{id:`b2-fb-107`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`'Could have + past participle' describes a past possibility that we didn't choose: We could have stayed longer.`,wrongReasons:{must:`'must have' is a deduction, but here it was an option we didn't take.`,can:`'can have' is not used for past possibilities; use 'could have'.`},text:`We ___ have taken a taxi, but the walk only took ten minutes, so we didn't bother.`,answers:[[`could`,`might`]]},{id:`b2-fb-108`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`'Can' has no future form, so use 'will be able to': I'll be able to help tomorrow.`,wrongReasons:{can:`'will can' is impossible; use 'will be able to'.`,been:`After 'will' use the base form 'be', not 'been'.`},text:`Will you ___ able to come to the party on Saturday?`,answers:[[`be`]]},{id:`b2-fb-109`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`Use 'can't' when evidence shows something is impossible: That can't be true.`,wrongReasons:{"mustn't":`'mustn't' expresses prohibition, not impossibility.`,"doesn't":`'doesn't be' is not grammatical; for a negative deduction use 'can't be'.`},text:`That ___ be right. I saw him at the station myself ten minutes ago.`,answers:[[`can't`,`cannot`,`couldn't`,`could not`]]},{id:`b2-fb-110`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`'Put off' means postpone or delay. Example: We put off the trip until spring.`,wrongReasons:{called:`'Call off' means cancel completely; here the meeting will still happen later.`,taken:`'Take off' does not mean postpone; it is used for planes or clothes.`},text:`The meeting has been ___ off until next Thursday because the manager is ill.`,answers:[[`put`]]},{id:`b2-fb-111`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`'Turn down' means reduce the volume. Example: Please turn the TV down.`,wrongReasons:{off:`'Turn off' stops the music completely, which does not match 'a little'.`,up:`'Turn up' makes the music louder, the opposite of what the speaker wants.`},text:`Could you turn the music ___ a little? I can hardly hear myself think.`,answers:[[`down`]]},{id:`b2-fb-112`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`'Put out' a fire means make it stop burning. Example: He put out the candle.`,wrongReasons:{off:`'Put off' means postpone; it does not mean stop a fire.`,down:`'Put down' means place something on a surface, not stop a fire.`},text:`The firefighters managed to put ___ the fire before it reached the forest.`,answers:[[`out`]]},{id:`b2-fb-113`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`'Come across' means find something by chance. Example: I came across an old letter in a drawer.`,wrongReasons:{over:`'Come over' means visit someone's home, not find something.`,into:`'Come into' means enter or inherit; it is not used for finding objects by chance.`},text:`I came ___ an old photo of my grandparents while I was clearing the attic.`,answers:[[`across`,`upon`]]},{id:`b2-fb-114`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`'Break down' means fail or stop working, for talks or machines. Example: The car broke down on the motorway.`,wrongReasons:{up:`'Break up' is used for relationships or groups ending, not talks failing.`,out:`'Break out' means start suddenly, like a war or a fire.`},text:`The peace talks broke ___ when neither side was willing to compromise.`,answers:[[`down`]]},{id:`b2-fb-115`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`'Run out of' something means have none left. Example: The printer has run out of paper.`,wrongReasons:{off:`'Run off' means leave quickly; 'run off of milk' is not English.`,away:`'Run away' means escape; it is not used for having none left.`},text:`We've run ___ of milk, so I'll go to the shop on the corner.`,answers:[[`out`]]},{id:`b2-fb-116`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`'Take after' means look or behave like an older family member. Example: He takes after his mother.`,wrongReasons:{up:`'Take up' means start a hobby; it is not about looking or acting like someone.`,on:`'Take on' means accept work or employ people, not resemble a relative.`},text:`She really takes ___ her father: they have the same laugh and the same sense of humour.`,answers:[[`after`]]},{id:`b2-fb-117`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`'Lay off' means stop employing workers because there is not enough work. Example: The airline laid off 500 staff.`,wrongReasons:{down:`'Lay down' means put something down or establish rules, not dismiss workers.`,out:`'Lay out' means arrange or spread something, not end someone's job.`},text:`When sales fell sharply, the factory had to lay ___ two hundred workers.`,answers:[[`off`]]},{id:`b2-fb-118`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`'Give up' + -ing means stop doing something. Example: She gave up eating meat last year.`,wrongReasons:{off:`'Give off' means produce smoke, smell or heat; it does not mean stop a habit.`,away:`'Give away' means give something for free, not stop doing it.`},text:`He tried to give ___ smoking three times before he finally managed to quit.`,answers:[[`up`]]},{id:`b2-fb-119`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`'Find out' means discover information. Example: I found out that the shop was closed.`,wrongReasons:{about:`'Find about' is not a phrasal verb; we say 'find out about' a topic.`,up:`'Find up' does not exist in English.`},text:`Scientists are still trying to find ___ why so many bees are disappearing.`,answers:[[`out`]]},{id:`b2-fb-120`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`'Take off' is used when a plane leaves the ground. Example: The flight took off at noon.`,wrongReasons:{out:`'Take out' means remove or borrow, not leave the ground.`,up:`'Take up' means start a hobby or use space, not a plane leaving.`},text:`Because of the thick fog, the plane took ___ two hours late.`,answers:[[`off`]]},{id:`b2-fb-121`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`'Look something up' means find information in a book or online. Example: I looked up the address.`,wrongReasons:{for:`'Look for' means search; it cannot go after the object like 'look it for'.`,out:`'Look out' means be careful; it is not used for checking a dictionary.`},text:`If you don't know what a word means, look it ___ in a dictionary.`,answers:[[`up`]]},{id:`b2-fb-122`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`'Look into' means investigate or examine a problem. Example: The manager will look into your complaint.`,wrongReasons:{after:`'Look after' means take care of someone, not investigate.`,at:`'Look at' means direct your eyes towards something; it is weaker than investigate.`},text:`The police are looking ___ the robbery at the jeweller's shop.`,answers:[[`into`]]},{id:`b2-fb-123`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`'Put up with' means accept something unpleasant without complaining. Example: I can't put up with his rudeness.`,wrongReasons:{on:`'Put on' is used for clothes or weight, not for accepting something annoying.`,down:`'Put down with' is not a phrase in English.`},text:`Our neighbours' dog barked every night, and in the end we couldn't put ___ with the noise any longer.`,answers:[[`up`]]},{id:`b2-fb-124`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`'Pull down' a building means destroy it. Example: They pulled down the old factory.`,wrongReasons:{out:`'Pull out' means leave or withdraw, not destroy a building.`,over:`'Pull over' is used when a car moves to the side of the road.`},text:`The old cinema was pulled ___ to make room for a new car park.`,answers:[[`down`]]},{id:`b2-fb-125`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`'Go off' is used when an alarm or bomb suddenly starts or explodes. Example: My alarm clock went off at six.`,wrongReasons:{on:`'Go on' means continue; it does not describe an alarm ringing.`,out:`'Go out' means stop burning or leave home, the opposite of an alarm starting.`},text:`When the fire alarm went ___, everyone left the building calmly.`,answers:[[`off`]]},{id:`b2-fb-126`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`'Pick someone up' means collect them, usually by car. Example: I'll pick the kids up after school.`,wrongReasons:{out:`'Pick out' means choose, not collect someone by car.`,off:`'Pick off' means remove small things one by one, not collect a person.`},text:`Can you pick me ___ from the station at six? My suitcase is really heavy.`,answers:[[`up`]]},{id:`b2-fb-127`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`'Fall through' means a plan or deal fails and does not happen. Example: Our holiday plans fell through.`,wrongReasons:{down:`'Fall down' means drop to the ground, not fail to happen.`,apart:`'Fall apart' means break into pieces; we say a deal 'falls through'.`},text:`The sale of the house fell ___ at the last minute when the buyer changed his mind.`,answers:[[`through`]]},{id:`b2-fb-128`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`linkers`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`'Despite' or 'in spite of' + noun shows contrast. Example: Despite the cold, we went swimming.`,wrongReasons:{although:`'Although' must be followed by a clause with a verb, not just a noun.`,however:`'However' connects two sentences; it cannot come before a noun like this.`},text:`___ the heavy rain, the match went ahead as planned.`,answers:[[`despite`,`in spite of`]]},{id:`b2-fb-129`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`linkers`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`'However' or 'nevertheless' at the start of a sentence shows contrast with the previous one. Example: It was late. However, we kept going.`,wrongReasons:{although:`'Although' cannot start a sentence followed by a comma; it needs a clause after it.`,despite:`'Despite' needs a noun or -ing form after it, not a comma.`},text:`The hotel was rather expensive. ___, the service was excellent.`,answers:[[`however`,`nevertheless`,`nonetheless`,`even so`,`still`]]},{id:`b2-fb-130`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`linkers`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`'So that' + clause shows purpose. Example: Speak louder so that everyone can hear.`,wrongReasons:{as:`'So as' is followed by 'to' + infinitive, not by a clause with a subject.`,to:`'So to' is not correct English; use 'so that' + clause.`},text:`She left home early so ___ she wouldn't miss her flight.`,answers:[[`that`]]},{id:`b2-fb-131`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`linkers`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`'As long as' or 'provided' introduces a condition. Example: You can stay as long as you're quiet.`,wrongReasons:{unless:`'Unless' means 'if not', so the meaning would be the opposite.`,"in case":`'In case' is about preparing for a possible event, not a condition.`},text:`I'll lend you my car ___ you promise to drive carefully.`,answers:[[`as long as`,`so long as`,`provided`,`providing`,`provided that`,`providing that`,`on condition that`,`if`]]},{id:`b2-fb-132`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`linkers`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`'In case' means as a precaution because something might happen. Example: Write it down in case you forget.`,wrongReasons:{order:`'In order' is followed by 'to' or 'that' and shows purpose, not a precaution.`,fact:`'In fact' adds information; it cannot join a precaution to a possible event.`},text:`Take an umbrella in ___ it rains this afternoon.`,answers:[[`case`]]},{id:`b2-fb-133`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`linkers`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`'Although' + clause shows contrast. Example: Although it was cold, she wore a T-shirt.`,wrongReasons:{despite:`'Despite' needs a noun or -ing form, not a full clause.`,however:`'However' cannot join two clauses in one sentence like this.`},text:`___ he was exhausted, he kept working until midnight.`,answers:[[`although`,`though`,`even though`]]},{id:`b2-fb-134`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`linkers`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`'Unless' means 'if not'. Example: We'll be late unless we hurry.`,wrongReasons:{if:`'If' would give the opposite meaning: studying more would make you fail.`,until:`'Until' is about time; it does not express this condition naturally here.`},text:`You won't pass the exam ___ you start studying more seriously.`,answers:[[`unless`]]},{id:`b2-fb-135`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`linkers`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`'Due to' or 'owing to' + noun gives a reason. Example: The game was stopped due to bad light.`,wrongReasons:{because:`'Because' needs 'of' before a noun, not 'to'.`,thanks:`'Thanks to' is used for good results, not for cancellations.`},text:`All flights were cancelled ___ to a strike by air traffic controllers.`,answers:[[`due`,`owing`]]},{id:`b2-fb-136`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`linkers`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`'So as (not) to' + infinitive shows purpose. Example: We whispered so as not to wake the baby.`,wrongReasons:{that:`'So that' needs a subject and verb after it, not 'not to'.`,much:`'So much' is about quantity and does not express purpose.`},text:`He wore a thick coat so ___ not to feel the cold.`,answers:[[`as`]]},{id:`b2-fb-137`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`linkers`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`'Whereas' or 'while' compares two different facts. Example: He likes tea, whereas I prefer coffee.`,wrongReasons:{however:`'However' cannot join two clauses after a comma; it needs a new sentence or a semicolon.`,despite:`'Despite' must be followed by a noun or -ing form, not a clause.`},text:`My sister is very organised, ___ I am always losing things.`,answers:[[`whereas`,`while`,`whilst`,`but`]]},{id:`b2-fb-138`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`linkers`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`The pattern is 'not only ... but also'. Example: Not only is she clever, but she is also kind.`,wrongReasons:{and:`After 'not only', the second part uses 'but (also)', not 'and'.`,so:`'So' shows result, which does not complete the pattern 'not only ... but also'.`},text:`Not only was the new phone faster, ___ it was also cheaper.`,answers:[[`but`]]},{id:`b2-fb-139`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`linkers`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`'As well as' means 'and also'. Example: He plays the guitar as well as the piano.`,wrongReasons:{good:`The fixed phrase is 'as well as', not 'as good as', which compares quality.`,much:`'As much as' compares amounts; it does not mean 'and also'.`},text:`She speaks fluent French and Spanish, as ___ as a little Japanese.`,answers:[[`well`]]},{id:`b2-fb-140`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`linkers`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`'According to' + source tells us where information comes from. Example: According to the report, prices will rise.`,wrongReasons:{accordingly:`'Accordingly' is an adverb meaning 'so'; it cannot be followed by 'to'.`,thanks:`'Thanks to' gives a cause of something good, not the source of information.`},text:`___ to the weather forecast, it's going to snow tomorrow.`,answers:[[`according`]]},{id:`b2-fb-141`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`linkers`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`'Such as' introduces examples. Example: I enjoy sports such as tennis and golf.`,wrongReasons:{like:`We say 'such as' or 'like', but never 'like as'.`,so:`'So as' expresses purpose, not examples.`},text:`I've visited many countries, ___ as Peru, Kenya and Vietnam.`,answers:[[`such`]]},{id:`b2-fb-142`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`linkers`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`'But' or 'yet' joins two contrasting ideas. Example: It was expensive, but we bought it.`,wrongReasons:{so:`'So' shows a result, but going to work is a surprising contrast.`,despite:`'Despite' cannot be followed by a full clause like 'she still went'.`},text:`She had a high temperature, ___ she still went to work.`,answers:[[`but`,`yet`]]},{id:`b2-fb-143`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`linkers`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`'In order to' + infinitive shows purpose. Example: She got up early in order to catch the train.`,wrongReasons:{case:`'In case' means as a precaution, and it is not followed by 'to'.`,purpose:`'In purpose to' is not English; the phrase is 'in order to'.`},text:`He saved money every month in ___ to buy his own house.`,answers:[[`order`]]},{id:`b2-fb-144`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`linkers`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`'As a result' shows a consequence. Example: He trained hard. As a result, he won.`,wrongReasons:{for:`The fixed phrase is 'as a result', not 'for a result'.`,with:`'With a result' is not a linking phrase in English.`},text:`The tickets sold out within minutes. ___ a result, many fans were disappointed.`,answers:[[`as`]]},{id:`b2-fb-145`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`linkers`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`'Because of' + noun gives a reason. Example: The road was closed because of the snow.`,wrongReasons:{to:`'Because to' is wrong; 'because' is followed by 'of' before a noun.`,for:`'Because for' is not English; use 'because of'.`},text:`We stayed indoors because ___ the storm.`,answers:[[`of`]]},{id:`b2-fb-146`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`verb-patterns`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`'Be used to' + -ing means be familiar with something. Example: She is used to working at night.`,wrongReasons:{for:`The phrase is 'be used to' + -ing, not 'used for'.`,with:`'Used with' is not the right preposition for being familiar with something.`},text:`I'm not used ___ driving on the left, so I feel a bit nervous.`,answers:[[`to`]]},{id:`b2-fb-147`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`verb-patterns`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`'Admit' + -ing (or 'having' + past participle). Example: She admitted taking the money.`,wrongReasons:{"to have":`'Admit' is followed by the -ing form, not 'to' + infinitive.`,to:`'Admit to' needs an -ing form after it, so 'to broken' is wrong.`},text:`He admitted ___ broken the window and offered to pay for it.`,answers:[[`having`]]},{id:`b2-fb-148`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`verb-patterns`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`'Would you mind' + -ing is a polite request. Example: Would you mind opening the window?`,wrongReasons:{"to close":`'Mind' is followed by the -ing form, not 'to' + infinitive.`,close:`After 'mind', the verb must be in the -ing form.`},text:`Would you mind ___ the door? There's a terrible draught in here.`,answers:[[`closing`,`shutting`]]},{id:`b2-fb-149`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`verb-patterns`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`'Let' + object + infinitive without 'to'. Example: My boss let me leave early.`,wrongReasons:{"to stay":`'Let' is followed by the infinitive without 'to'.`,staying:`'Let' takes the bare infinitive, not the -ing form.`},text:`When I was a teenager, my parents never let me ___ out after ten o'clock.`,answers:[[`stay`,`go`]]},{id:`b2-fb-150`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`verb-patterns`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`'Make' + object + bare infinitive means force. Example: The teacher made us rewrite the essay.`,wrongReasons:{"to apologise":`In the active voice, 'make' + object takes the infinitive without 'to'.`,apologising:`'Make' + object is not followed by the -ing form.`},text:`She made her son ___ to the neighbour for the broken window.`,answers:[[`apologise`,`apologize`]]},{id:`b2-fb-151`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`verb-patterns`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`'Regret' + -ing talks about something in the past you are sorry about. Example: I regret not saying goodbye.`,wrongReasons:{"to study":`'Regret' + 'to' is used for giving bad news, not for past actions.`,study:`After 'regret not', the verb needs the -ing form.`},text:`I regret not ___ harder at school; now I wish I had better qualifications.`,answers:[[`studying`,`working`]]},{id:`b2-fb-152`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`verb-patterns`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`'Remember to' + infinitive means do not forget a future task. Example: Remember to buy some bread.`,wrongReasons:{for:`'Remember for' is not followed by a verb; use 'remember to' + infinitive.`,of:`'Remember of' is not correct English.`},text:`Remember ___ lock the back door when you leave the house.`,answers:[[`to`]]},{id:`b2-fb-153`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`verb-patterns`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`'It's no use' + -ing means it is pointless. Example: It's no use waiting; she won't come.`,wrongReasons:{"to cry":`'It's no use' is followed by the -ing form, not 'to' + infinitive.`,cry:`After 'no use', the verb must be in the -ing form.`},text:`It's no use ___ over spilt milk; the money is gone, so let's move on.`,answers:[[`crying`]]},{id:`b2-fb-154`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`verb-patterns`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`'Manage to' + infinitive means succeed in doing something difficult. Example: He managed to fix the car.`,wrongReasons:{for:`'Manage' is followed by 'to' + infinitive, not 'for'.`,in:`'Manage in' is not used before a verb; we say 'manage to do'.`},text:`We managed ___ get tickets for the concert just before they sold out.`,answers:[[`to`]]},{id:`b2-fb-155`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`verb-patterns`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`'Good at' + -ing describes a skill. Example: She is good at solving puzzles.`,wrongReasons:{in:`The adjective 'good' takes 'at' for skills, not 'in'.`,on:`'Good on' is not used for abilities; we say 'good at'.`},text:`My uncle is very good ___ repairing things around the house.`,answers:[[`at`]]},{id:`b2-fb-156`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`verb-patterns`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`The negative infinitive is 'not to' + verb. Example: She promised not to tell anyone.`,wrongReasons:{no:`'No' cannot make an infinitive negative; use 'not to'.`,"don't":`'Don't' cannot be used before 'to' + infinitive.`},text:`They decided ___ to wait any longer and went home.`,answers:[[`not`]]},{id:`b2-fb-157`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`verb-patterns`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`'Would rather' + bare infinitive expresses preference. Example: I'd rather walk than take the bus.`,wrongReasons:{"to stay":`'Would rather' is followed by the infinitive without 'to'.`,staying:`'Would rather' does not take the -ing form.`},text:`Would you rather ___ at home tonight or go out for dinner?`,answers:[[`stay`,`eat`,`be`]]},{id:`b2-fb-158`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`verb-patterns`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`'Warn someone not to' + infinitive. Example: They warned him not to swim there.`,wrongReasons:{"don't":`'Warn' + object + 'not to' is correct; 'don't to' is not English.`,no:`'No to' is wrong; the negative infinitive uses 'not'.`},text:`The teacher warned us ___ to be late for the exam again.`,answers:[[`not`]]},{id:`b2-fb-159`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`verb-patterns`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`'Can't help' + -ing means you cannot stop yourself. Example: I couldn't help laughing.`,wrongReasons:{"to feel":`'Can't help' is followed by the -ing form.`,feel:`After 'can't help', use the -ing form, not the base verb.`},text:`I can't help ___ sorry for him; he's had such a terrible year.`,answers:[[`feeling`]]},{id:`b2-fb-160`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`verb-patterns`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`'Consider' + -ing. Example: We are considering moving to the coast.`,wrongReasons:{"to leave":`'Consider' is followed by the -ing form, not 'to' + infinitive.`,leave:`After 'consider', the verb takes the -ing form.`},text:`She is seriously considering ___ her job and travelling around Asia.`,answers:[[`leaving`,`quitting`]]},{id:`b2-fb-161`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`verb-patterns`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`'Stop to' + infinitive means stop one action in order to do another. Example: We stopped to buy petrol.`,wrongReasons:{for:`'Stop for' is followed by a noun, like 'stop for lunch', not a verb.`,and:`'Stopped and answer' mixes tenses; 'stop to' + infinitive shows the reason.`},text:`He stopped ___ answer his phone, then carried on walking up the hill.`,answers:[[`to`]]},{id:`b2-fb-162`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`verb-patterns`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`'There's no point in' + -ing means it is useless. Example: There's no point in arguing.`,wrongReasons:{to:`The phrase is 'there's no point in' + -ing, not 'to'.`,of:`'No point of' is not correct; use 'no point in'.`},text:`There's no point ___ waiting any longer; the last train has already gone.`,answers:[[`in`]]},{id:`b2-fb-163`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`verb-patterns`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`'Encourage someone to' + infinitive. Example: My parents encouraged me to learn the piano.`,wrongReasons:{for:`'Encourage' + object is followed by 'to' + infinitive, not 'for'.`,in:`'Encourage in' is not used before a verb.`},text:`The manager encouraged everyone ___ share their ideas at the meeting.`,answers:[[`to`]]},{id:`b2-fb-164`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`narrative-tenses`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`The past perfect (had + past participle) shows an action before another past action. Example: When we got there, the film had started.`,wrongReasons:{has:`'Has' is present perfect; the story is in the past, so we need 'had'.`,was:`'Was left' would be passive and change the meaning.`},text:`When I arrived at the station, the train ___ already left, so I had to wait an hour.`,answers:[[`had`]]},{id:`b2-fb-165`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`narrative-tenses`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`The past continuous describes a background action that was in progress. Example: While I was cooking, the phone rang.`,wrongReasons:{was:`The subject 'we' needs 'were', not 'was'.`,are:`The story is in the past, so the present 'are' does not fit.`},text:`While we ___ walking along the beach, we found a message in a bottle.`,answers:[[`were`]]},{id:`b2-fb-166`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`narrative-tenses`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`The past perfect continuous (had been + -ing) shows a longer action before a past moment. Example: His eyes were red because he had been crying.`,wrongReasons:{has:`The situation is in the past, so the past perfect 'had been' is needed.`,was:`'Was been' is not a correct verb form.`},text:`She looked exhausted because she ___ been working in the garden all afternoon.`,answers:[[`had`]]},{id:`b2-fb-167`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`narrative-tenses`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`Use the past continuous for an action interrupted by a shorter past action. Example: She was taking a shower when the doorbell rang.`,wrongReasons:{were:`The subject 'I' takes 'was' in the past continuous.`,am:`The story is in the past, so the present 'am' is wrong.`},text:`I ___ reading in bed when the lights suddenly went out.`,answers:[[`was`]]},{id:`b2-fb-168`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`narrative-tenses`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`'By the time' + past simple is often followed by the past perfect. Example: By the time I woke up, everyone had left.`,wrongReasons:{have:`'Have escaped' is present perfect and does not fit a past story.`,were:`'Were escaped' is not correct; 'escape' needs the auxiliary 'had' here.`},text:`By the time the police arrived, the thieves ___ escaped through the back window.`,answers:[[`had`]]},{id:`b2-fb-169`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`narrative-tenses`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`Use 'had been' + -ing to explain the cause of a past situation. Example: The grass was wet because it had been raining.`,wrongReasons:{has:`The streets 'were' flooded, so the past perfect 'had' is needed, not 'has'.`,was:`'Was been' is not a possible verb form.`},text:`The streets were flooded; it ___ been raining heavily all night.`,answers:[[`had`]]},{id:`b2-fb-170`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`narrative-tenses`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`Use the past perfect with 'never ... before' for experiences up to a past moment. Example: I had never flown before that trip.`,wrongReasons:{has:`He moved in the past, so we need the past perfect 'had', not 'has'.`,did:`'Did never seen' is wrong; 'seen' needs the auxiliary 'had'.`},text:`My grandfather ___ never seen snow before he moved to Canada.`,answers:[[`had`]]},{id:`b2-fb-171`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`narrative-tenses`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`Use the past continuous to ask about an action in progress at a past time. Example: What were you doing at midnight?`,wrongReasons:{did:`'Did you doing' is wrong; the -ing form needs 'were'.`,was:`'You' takes 'were', not 'was'.`},text:`What ___ you doing at eight o'clock last night? I called you twice.`,answers:[[`were`]]},{id:`b2-fb-172`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`narrative-tenses`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`The past perfect can show that one action was completed before another. Example: As soon as he had paid, he left the shop.`,wrongReasons:{has:`The story is in the past, so 'has finished' does not fit.`,was:`'Was finished' is passive and does not fit with 'she' doing the action.`},text:`As soon as she ___ finished her speech, the audience began to clap.`,answers:[[`had`]]},{id:`b2-fb-173`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`narrative-tenses`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`Use the past continuous to describe the background scene of a story. Example: It was raining and people were hurrying home.`,wrongReasons:{was:`The plural subject 'the birds' needs 'were'.`,are:`The scene is in the past, so the present 'are' does not fit.`},text:`The sun was shining and the birds ___ singing as we set off on our walk.`,answers:[[`were`]]},{id:`b2-fb-174`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`narrative-tenses`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`The past perfect explains a change that happened before a past moment. Example: She had cut her hair, so I didn't know her.`,wrongReasons:{has:`The past simple 'didn't recognise' requires the past perfect 'had grown'.`,was:`'Was grown' is passive and does not fit here.`},text:`I didn't recognise him at first because he ___ grown a long beard.`,answers:[[`had`]]},{id:`b2-fb-175`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`narrative-tenses`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`'It was the first time' is followed by the past perfect. Example: It was the first time she had seen the sea.`,wrongReasons:{have:`After 'It was the first time', use the past perfect 'had', not 'have'.`,did:`'Did ever tasted' is wrong; 'tasted' needs the auxiliary 'had'.`},text:`It was the first time I ___ ever tasted Japanese food.`,answers:[[`had`]]},{id:`b2-fb-176`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`narrative-tenses`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`Use the past perfect continuous to ask about duration up to a past moment. Example: How long had they been living there when the war started?`,wrongReasons:{have:`The bus came in the past, so we need 'had been', not 'have been'.`,were:`'Were you been' is not a correct verb form.`},text:`How long ___ you been waiting when the bus finally came?`,answers:[[`had`]]},{id:`b2-fb-177`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`narrative-tenses`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`Use the past simple for a short completed action during a longer one. Example: I saw an accident while I was cycling home.`,wrongReasons:{"was hearing":`'Hear' is usually not used in the continuous form; a short completed action uses the past simple.`,"has heard":`The present perfect does not fit a story set in the past with 'was driving'.`},text:`She ___ the news on the radio while she was driving to work.`,answers:[[`heard`]]},{id:`b2-fb-178`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`narrative-tenses`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`Use the past perfect for something that happened before another past action. Example: I noticed I had forgotten my wallet.`,wrongReasons:{have:`After 'realised' (past), the earlier action needs 'had left'.`,was:`'Was left' is passive and changes the meaning.`},text:`When I got home, I realised I ___ left my keys at the office.`,answers:[[`had`]]},{id:`b2-fb-179`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`narrative-tenses`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`Use the past continuous for an action interrupted by another event. Example: We were having lunch when the storm began.`,wrongReasons:{was:`'The children' is plural, so it needs 'were'.`,had:`'Had playing' is not a correct verb form; use 'were playing' or 'had been playing'.`},text:`The children ___ playing football in the park when it started to hail.`,answers:[[`were`]]},{id:`b2-fb-180`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`narrative-tenses`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`Use the past simple for a short action that interrupts a longer one. Example: Just as we sat down, the doorbell rang.`,wrongReasons:{ringed:`'Ring' is irregular: the past simple is 'rang', not 'ringed'.`,rung:`'Rung' is the past participle; the past simple is 'rang'.`},text:`Just as I was leaving the house, the phone ___.`,answers:[[`rang`]]},{id:`b2-fb-181`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`narrative-tenses`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`'Had had' is the past perfect of 'have', used for an earlier past action. Example: After she had had a rest, she felt better.`,wrongReasons:{have:`The story is in the past, so 'have had' does not fit; use 'had had'.`,were:`'Were had' is not a correct verb form.`},text:`After they ___ had dinner, they went for a walk by the river.`,answers:[[`had`]]},{id:`b2-fb-182`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`future-forms`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`Use 'be going to' for predictions based on present evidence. Example: Watch out! You're going to fall.`,wrongReasons:{will:`'Will going to' is wrong; the form is 'be going to'.`,are:`The subject 'it' needs 'is', not 'are'.`},text:`Look at those dark clouds! It ___ going to rain any minute.`,answers:[[`is`]]},{id:`b2-fb-183`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`future-forms`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`The future continuous (will be + -ing) describes an action in progress at a future time. Example: At ten tomorrow, I'll be flying to Rome.`,wrongReasons:{am:`'Am be' is not English; the future continuous is 'will be' + -ing.`,would:`'Would' is used for conditions or the past, not a definite future plan.`},text:`This time next week, I ___ be lying on a beach in Greece.`,answers:[[`will`,`shall`]]},{id:`b2-fb-184`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`future-forms`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`The future perfect (will have + past participle) shows something completed before a future time. Example: By June, I will have finished my degree.`,wrongReasons:{are:`'Are have finished' is wrong; the future perfect is 'will have' + past participle.`,would:`'Would have finished' talks about an unreal past, not a future deadline.`},text:`By the end of next year, they ___ have finished building the new bridge.`,answers:[[`will`]]},{id:`b2-fb-185`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`future-forms`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`Use the present continuous (am/is/are + -ing) for fixed future arrangements. Example: I'm meeting Sarah tomorrow at noon.`,wrongReasons:{will:`'Will visiting' is wrong; 'will' needs the base form or 'will be visiting'.`,have:`'Have visiting' is not a correct verb form.`},text:`Sorry, I can't come to the party on Saturday; I ___ visiting my grandmother.`,answers:[[`am`]]},{id:`b2-fb-186`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`future-forms`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`Use 'will' for offers and decisions made at the moment of speaking. Example: You look tired; I'll make dinner.`,wrongReasons:{do:`'I do do them' is not a natural offer; use 'will' for an offer made now.`,am:`'Am do' is not English; 'am' needs 'going to' or an -ing form.`},text:`Don't worry about the dishes. I ___ do them later.`,answers:[[`will`,`shall`]]},{id:`b2-fb-187`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`future-forms`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`Use the future perfect for an action completed before a future moment. Example: The guests will have left by midnight.`,wrongReasons:{will:`'Will started' is wrong; we need the future perfect 'will have started'.`,has:`'Has started' is present perfect; with 'by the time we get', use 'will have'.`},text:`The film ___ started by the time we get to the cinema, so let's hurry.`,answers:[[`will have`]]},{id:`b2-fb-188`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`future-forms`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`'Be about to' + infinitive means something will happen very soon. Example: The film is about to start.`,wrongReasons:{for:`'About for' is wrong; the form is 'be about to' + infinitive.`,of:`'About of' is not English.`},text:`We're about ___ leave, so if you want a lift, get your coat now.`,answers:[[`to`]]},{id:`b2-fb-189`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`future-forms`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`Use the future perfect with 'for' to show duration up to a future time. Example: In May, I'll have lived here for ten years.`,wrongReasons:{are:`'Are have been' is wrong; the future perfect needs 'will have'.`,would:`'Would' is not used for a fact that will become true at a future date.`},text:`Next spring, my parents ___ have been married for forty years.`,answers:[[`will`]]},{id:`b2-fb-190`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`future-forms`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`Use the present simple for timetabled future events. Example: The train leaves at 8.30.`,wrongReasons:{is:`'Is the concert start' is wrong; 'is' would need 'starting'.`,do:`'The concert' is singular, so it needs 'does', not 'do'.`},text:`What time ___ the concert start tomorrow evening?`,answers:[[`does`,`will`]]},{id:`b2-fb-191`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`future-forms`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`After time words like 'as soon as' and 'when', use the present simple for the future. Example: I'll tell her when I see her.`,wrongReasons:{"will arrive":`After 'as soon as' we use the present simple, not 'will', for the future.`,arrived:`The past simple does not fit a future event.`},text:`I'll phone you as soon as I ___ at the hotel.`,answers:[[`arrive`,`get`]]},{id:`b2-fb-192`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`future-forms`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`Use 'be going to' for plans and intentions. Example: She's going to start a new job in May.`,wrongReasons:{will:`'Will going to' is wrong; the form is 'be going to'.`,has:`'Has going to' is not a correct verb form.`},text:`Mark ___ going to study medicine next year; he has already been accepted.`,answers:[[`is`]]},{id:`b2-fb-193`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`future-forms`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`The future continuous shows an activity in progress at a future time. Example: At nine, I'll be working.`,wrongReasons:{are:`'Are be having' is wrong; the future continuous is 'will be' + -ing.`,would:`'Would be having' sounds conditional, not a planned future activity.`},text:`Please don't call between eight and ten tonight; we ___ be having dinner with friends.`,answers:[[`will`,`shall`]]},{id:`b2-fb-194`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`future-forms`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`'Be due to' + infinitive talks about planned or timetabled events. Example: The plane is due to land at six.`,wrongReasons:{about:`'About to' means very soon; it is not used with a specific time like 'at three o'clock'.`,able:`'Able to' talks about ability, not about a planned time.`},text:`The match is ___ to begin at three o'clock, but it may be delayed by the rain.`,answers:[[`due`,`scheduled`,`set`,`expected`,`supposed`,`planned`,`going`]]},{id:`b2-fb-195`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`future-forms`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`'Be about to' means very soon. Example: Quick, the bus is about to leave.`,wrongReasons:{will:`'Will about to' is wrong; the form is 'be about to'.`,are:`'The shop' is singular, so it needs 'is'.`},text:`Hurry up! The shop ___ about to close.`,answers:[[`is`]]},{id:`b2-fb-196`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`future-forms`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`Use 'will' / 'won't' for promises. Example: I won't be late again, I promise.`,wrongReasons:{"don't":`'Don't' is present simple; a promise about the future uses 'won't'.`,not:`'I not tell' is wrong; the negative future needs 'will not'.`},text:`I promise I ___ tell anyone your secret.`,answers:[[`won't`,`will not`,`shan't`,`shall not`]]},{id:`b2-fb-197`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`future-forms`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`After 'before', 'after' and 'until', use the present simple for the future. Example: Wash your hands before you eat.`,wrongReasons:{"will start":`After 'before' we use the present simple, not 'will', for the future.`,started:`The past simple does not fit an instruction about the future.`},text:`Before you ___ the exam, read all the questions carefully.`,answers:[[`start`,`begin`]]},{id:`b2-fb-198`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`future-forms`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`Use 'will' for predictions based on opinion, often after 'I'm sure' or 'I think'. Example: I think it will be a great match.`,wrongReasons:{are:`'Are love' is wrong; predictions based on opinion use 'will' + base form.`,would:`'Would love' expresses a wish or condition, not a confident prediction.`},text:`I'm sure you ___ love the new restaurant; the food is amazing.`,answers:[[`will`]]},{id:`b2-fb-199`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`vocabulary`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`'Do someone a favour' means help them. Example: Can you do me a favour and feed my cat?`,wrongReasons:{help:`We say 'do me a favour', not 'do me a help'.`,service:`'Do me a service' is not the usual phrase for a small request.`},text:`Could you do me a ___ and post this letter on your way to work?`,answers:[[`favour`,`favor`]]},{id:`b2-fb-200`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`vocabulary`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`Common collocation: make (or take) a decision. Example: It's hard to make a decision when you're tired.`,wrongReasons:{do:`We 'make' or 'take' a decision; we do not 'do' one.`,give:`'Give a decision' is not the natural collocation.`},text:`The committee needs to ___ a decision by Friday at the latest.`,answers:[[`make`,`take`,`reach`]]},{id:`b2-fb-201`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`vocabulary`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`Collocation: make progress. Example: The patient is making good progress.`,wrongReasons:{done:`We say 'make progress', not 'do progress'.`,got:`'Got progress' is not a natural collocation.`},text:`She has ___ a lot of progress with her Spanish this year.`,answers:[[`made`]]},{id:`b2-fb-202`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`vocabulary`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`Collocation: pay attention (to). Example: Please pay attention to the speaker.`,wrongReasons:{put:`The collocation is 'pay attention', not 'put attention'.`,make:`'Make attention' is not English.`},text:`It's important to ___ attention to the safety instructions before the flight.`,answers:[[`pay`]]},{id:`b2-fb-203`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`vocabulary`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`Collocation: make a mistake. Example: Everyone makes mistakes when learning a language.`,wrongReasons:{did:`We 'make' a mistake; we do not 'do' one.`,had:`'Had a mistake' is not the usual way to say someone caused an error.`},text:`The accountant ___ a mistake in the calculation, so the total was wrong.`,answers:[[`made`]]},{id:`b2-fb-204`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`vocabulary`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`Collocation: heavy traffic. Example: There is always heavy traffic on Friday evenings.`,wrongReasons:{strong:`We describe traffic as 'heavy', not 'strong'.`,big:`'Big traffic' is not a natural collocation in English.`},text:`The traffic was so ___ that it took us two hours to get home.`,answers:[[`heavy`,`bad`,`terrible`,`awful`,`slow`]]},{id:`b2-fb-205`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`vocabulary`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`Collocation: give / set homework (teacher), do homework (student). Example: She set the class an essay.`,wrongReasons:{makes:`Teachers 'give' or 'set' homework; they do not 'make' us homework.`,does:`Students 'do' homework, but teachers do not 'do' it for them.`},text:`Our science teacher always ___ us a lot of homework at the weekend.`,answers:[[`gives`,`sets`]]},{id:`b2-fb-206`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`vocabulary`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`Collocation: take a deep breath. Example: Take a deep breath and relax.`,wrongReasons:{made:`We say 'take a deep breath', not 'make' one.`,did:`'Did a deep breath' is not English.`},text:`Before the job interview, she ___ a deep breath and knocked on the door.`,answers:[[`took`]]},{id:`b2-fb-207`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`vocabulary`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`'Cut something short' means end it earlier than planned. Example: We cut our holiday short because of the storm.`,wrongReasons:{make:`The phrase is 'cut something short', not 'make it short' in this meaning.`,stop:`'Stop the meeting short' is not a natural expression.`},text:`We had to ___ the meeting short because the fire alarm went off.`,answers:[[`cut`]]},{id:`b2-fb-208`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`vocabulary`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`'Make sure' means check that something is done. Example: Make sure you have your passport.`,wrongReasons:{do:`The phrase is 'make sure', not 'do sure'.`,be:`'Be sure that' needs a different structure; the fixed phrase here is 'make sure'.`},text:`Please ___ sure that all the windows are closed before you leave.`,answers:[[`make`]]},{id:`b2-fb-209`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`vocabulary`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`'Make fun of' someone means laugh at them unkindly. Example: Don't make fun of your little brother.`,wrongReasons:{did:`The phrase is 'make fun of', not 'do fun of'.`,had:`'Had fun of' is wrong; 'have fun' means enjoy yourself.`},text:`The other boys ___ fun of him because of his accent, which was very unkind.`,answers:[[`made`]]},{id:`b2-fb-210`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`vocabulary`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`'Get a joke' means understand why it is funny. Example: Everyone laughed, but I didn't get it.`,wrongReasons:{take:`'Take the joke' is not used for understanding humour.`,know:`'Know the joke' means you have heard it before, not that you understand it.`},text:`Sorry, I didn't ___ the joke. Can you explain it?`,answers:[[`get`,`understand`]]},{id:`b2-fb-211`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`vocabulary`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`'Take effect' means start to apply or work. Example: The new speed limit takes effect next month.`,wrongReasons:{make:`The collocation is 'take effect', not 'make effect'.`,do:`'Do effect' is not English.`},text:`The new law will ___ effect from the first of January.`,answers:[[`take`]]},{id:`b2-fb-212`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`vocabulary`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`Collocation: have a good memory for something. Example: He has a terrible memory for names.`,wrongReasons:{remember:`'Remember' is a verb; after 'an excellent' we need a noun.`,memorise:`'Memorise' is a verb meaning learn by heart; we need a noun after 'an excellent'.`},text:`My grandmother has an excellent ___ for faces; she never forgets anyone she meets.`,answers:[[`memory`]]},{id:`b2-fb-213`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`vocabulary`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`Collocation: make a profit / make a loss. Example: The shop made a small profit in its first year.`,wrongReasons:{did:`Companies 'make' a profit; they do not 'do' one.`,won:`We 'win' prizes or games, not profits.`},text:`Despite the crisis, the company ___ a profit of two million dollars last year.`,answers:[[`made`,`earned`,`reported`,`posted`,`recorded`,`achieved`,`turned`]]},{id:`b2-fb-214`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`vocabulary`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`Collocations: find the time / spare the time. Example: I can't spare the time to go shopping today.`,wrongReasons:{do:`'Do the time' means spend time in prison, not have time to help.`,"make up":`'Make up the time' means recover lost time, which is a different meaning.`},text:`I'm afraid I can't ___ the time to help you this week; I'm far too busy.`,answers:[[`find`,`spare`,`make`]]},{id:`b2-fb-215`,type:`fill-blank`,level:`B2`,topic:`vocabulary`,prompt:`Complete the sentence.`,explanation:`Collocation: break a promise (opposite: keep a promise). Example: She never breaks her promises.`,wrongReasons:{cut:`We 'break' a promise; we do not 'cut' one.`,lost:`'Lose a promise' is not English.`},text:`He ___ his promise and never came back to repay the money.`,answers:[[`broke`]]}]};export{e as default};